#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/34/34184a95be2b3ae6865fde109ace7e8b4397f4b01982928be73cff4199792d61.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/b9/b9f56d3f0414f4b56b50e59c2c02d9072f1fb74ce09d801a1ab62abac61ad737.png)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/85/8562a693df46625ee0dc2bcdecc664dfc50a99889fdb855ae2e06dc1db02a3d4.png)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/d2/d263bfe2386309c55f1d63d97d15eaa682e9b0748a9bee6d3378d0b9035927ba.png)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/6c/6ccb00b5e1387cd15847c50d66b26ee903d95428bcff2bfc933b675f9b590717.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/e3/e300a9ac18f0170ae9053c9635fe13a9dd6def793ebfc0bd9dbe81e28810a347.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/f3/f3d533c5687da1a75a07c6ad86b40ee8aa6ea5c86cdd250d5c47ff99b885ef61.png)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5d/5d576fca72f6a4f9f0668ff0efbdc2cacc33f8dbb2c00192a7397da9ec83adea.png)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/4f/4f6a034def35760ae94b6eb6a425c59950556a78c124aa9ad0fcfdaa06053b28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/08/085cb01a2e4da286ea19a6f1e1707e685646af3d887abb1a57ab50e4d352c53f.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/d7/d7b93aa8abed895e49b965b8d0905088249475cad98757e7a93a174fae143b49.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](images/a2/a29e80b35c9505ee665a109978de9b936ee7ce59eef631d7af9a4b7954bd8696.png)" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d2/d2f89fe83787daf667e01fd4c4fe644bc36cfe23b046088c22e8de17bb02dc28.png)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a5acc6-4f22-572f-9a0b-3eaf9af02343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400424} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca486fb-064c-5b4e-8ab3-1a558d20ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400425} {:answer/id #uuid "b98b25bd-05dd-576c-8209-1bbc53b4383c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400426} {:answer/id #uuid "103497c7-00ac-524e-96db-dde3e3e299c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400427} {:answer/id #uuid "a355422c-4d74-55b5-a177-12f7fda4364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "103497c7-00ac-524e-96db-dde3e3e299c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400427} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24f1ce6-8404-5d4a-83e2-7f5ae99bc979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88771814-b2fc-53f4-914f-2f284d52aece" :question/math-academy-id "q-129222" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${a}_{43}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${b}_{43}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b620f2-8eab-56f7-bf71-ece0313b2c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259937} {:answer/id #uuid "0e92311e-c0e1-5dd6-a876-0f872e3a96cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260011} {:answer/id #uuid "3255efab-d3ed-5241-8a64-e81d51e7cf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260328} {:answer/id #uuid "5c74d1ad-b051-5673-95d3-6cf0087523e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260666} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbfc58b-72f2-5768-89a5-a816603a29ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbbfc58b-72f2-5768-89a5-a816603a29ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261670} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4a71031-b672-5d73-8aa3-dfec74de2c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5479435-9835-502a-bd24-1f955706f0c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-129228" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0624289a-8dfc-5c15-af8f-ba1cb4c34aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263054} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff2eaee-3fb7-558b-904e-b1aa73f1dfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263829} {:answer/id #uuid "af33b68d-5796-591c-9b65-1f0810f7895a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264513} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67339b-098a-579e-9a59-6b83f013016d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$ and ${a}_{32}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264789} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d9608c-c7fe-5edb-90bd-370db5125284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{43}$ and ${a}_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff2eaee-3fb7-558b-904e-b1aa73f1dfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263829} :answer-field/id #uuid "021f4039-528b-5a31-a6af-7977fed20985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4da5d683-7056-5520-8077-4c10803e9f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-129424" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e91a3af-86df-59c5-8a37-2f12fc58c402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339395} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd4dd8e-8964-56db-b148-79e66ceb0f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339531} {:answer/id #uuid "78519f26-f869-54b2-b128-24fc64cb9fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340202} {:answer/id #uuid "9e00dcce-1529-5935-b71c-eed477dc8d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340552} {:answer/id #uuid "bec360f7-3028-58e9-a136-96375a206547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e91a3af-86df-59c5-8a37-2f12fc58c402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339395} :answer-field/id #uuid "015d858b-54ff-587e-8c9f-8f990f164c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b6b5339-4232-5551-9dd0-10e8019e2d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-73143" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $4$ and $-1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $4+(-1)=3.$"}]}
